Chapter 3

Equilibrium Analysis

We now study the steady‑state behaviour of the isolated digester. Although the system is perpetually perturbed in practice, equilibrium analysis reveals the operating point that the ISS dynamics tend toward when inputs are held constant.

Let u = \bar{u} = (\bar{S}_{\text{in}}, \bar{q}_{\text{out}})^\top be a constant input vector. An equilibrium point x^* = (S^*, X^*, V^*, G^*)^\top satisfies

f(x^*, \bar{u}) = 0, \qquad x^* \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^4 .
\tag{3.1}

Theorem 3.1 (Existence). For any constant input \bar{u} \in \mathcal{U}, there exists at least one nonnegative equilibrium.

Proof.
Consider the reduced subsystem in the first three components, since G does not feed back into the dynamics. The equilibrium conditions for S, X, V are:

\begin{aligned}
0 &= D(\bar{S}_{\text{in}} - S) - k_1 \mu(S) X, \\
0 &= (\mu(S) - k_d - D) X, \\
0 &= k_2 \mu(S) X - D V.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.2}

From the second equation, either X = 0 or \mu(S) = k_d + D.

Case 1: X = 0. Then the first equation gives S = \bar{S}_{\text{in}}. The third gives V = 0. The fourth equation then yields 0 = Y \cdot 0 - \bar{q}_{\text{out}}, which forces \bar{q}_{\text{out}} = 0. Hence (S, X, V, G) = (\bar{S}_{\text{in}}, 0, 0, G^*) with G^* arbitrary? But the gas equation 0 = Y V - \bar{q}_{\text{out}} becomes 0 = 0 - \bar{q}_{\text{out}} so only possible if \bar{q}_{\text{out}} = 0; then G^* is free, but physically G^* = 0 if there is no production. In practice, a zero biomass equilibrium is a washout state that occurs only when no gas is extracted and no substrate is converted.

Case 2: X > 0. Then necessarily

\mu(S^*) = k_d + D .
\tag{3.3}

Because \mu is strictly increasing and bounded, its inverse exists on the range (\mu(0), \mu_{\max}). Since k_d + D is a positive constant, we can solve uniquely

S^* = \mu^{-1}(k_d + D),

provided that k_d + D < \mu_{\max} (otherwise no positive biomass can be sustained). This is a standard biological condition: the dilution rate plus death rate must not exceed the maximum growth rate.

Once S^* is fixed, we can solve for X from the first equation:

k_1 \mu(S^*) X = D(\bar{S}_{\text{in}} - S^*) \quad \Rightarrow \quad 
X^* = \frac{D}{k_1 (k_d + D)} (\bar{S}_{\text{in}} - S^*),

which is positive if and only if \bar{S}_{\text{in}} > S^*. Then V^* = \frac{k_2}{D} \mu(S^*) X^* = \frac{k_2}{D} (k_d + D) X^* > 0. Finally, from the gas equation 0 = Y V^* - \bar{q}_{\text{out}} we obtain \bar{q}_{\text{out}} = Y V^*, which determines the equilibrium gas extraction rate consistent with the steady state; if \bar{q}_{\text{out}} is specified independently, then we may not have an equilibrium, but in control design we adjust extraction to match production.

Thus an equilibrium exists (the positive one) under the natural biological conditions \bar{S}_{\text{in}} > S^* and k_d + D < \mu_{\max}. If these fail, the washout equilibrium is the only possibility. \square

Theorem 3.2 (Local uniqueness). Under the same conditions, when a positive equilibrium exists, it is locally unique.

Proof.
We examine the Jacobian J = \partial f / \partial x evaluated at x^*. The dynamics for S, X, V do not depend on G, so we can consider the 3\times 3 block. A routine calculation gives

J = \begin{pmatrix}
-D - k_1 \mu'(S^*) X^* & -k_1 \mu(S^*) & 0 \\
\mu'(S^*) X^* & 0 & 0 \\
k_2 \mu'(S^*) X^* & k_2 \mu(S^*) & -D
\end{pmatrix}.

Its determinant is

\det(J) = -D \left[ \bigl(-D - k_1 \mu' X^*\bigr)\cdot 0 - (-k_1 \mu(S^*))\cdot (\mu' X^*) \right] = -D \cdot ( -k_1 \mu(S^*) \mu'(S^*) X^* ).

Actually, expand: \det(J) = (-D - k_1 \mu' X^*)\cdot (0\cdot(-D) - 0\cdot k_2\mu) - (-k_1 \mu)\cdot (\mu' X^*\cdot(-D) - 0\cdot k_2\mu' X^*) + 0\cdot (\mu' X^*\cdot k_2\mu - 0\cdot (-D - k_1\mu' X^*)). Simplify: first term zero, second term (-k_1 \mu) \times ( -D \mu' X^*) = k_1 \mu D \mu' X^*. Thus \det(J) = k_1 D \mu(S^*) \mu'(S^*) X^*. At the positive equilibrium, \mu(S^*) = k_d + D > 0, X^* > 0, and \mu'(S^*) > 0. Hence \det(J) \neq 0. By the Implicit Function Theorem, the map x \mapsto f(x, \bar{u}) is locally invertible, guaranteeing that the solution of f(x, \bar{u}) = 0 is locally unique. \square

Remark 3.3. Global uniqueness is not asserted, and indeed the system can exhibit multiple equilibria in the washout regime. However, the ISS property ensures that trajectories starting in the positive orthant and subjected to realistic inputs remain bounded away from zero biomass if the substrate supply is adequate; the positive equilibrium is the only attractor of practical interest.

