Chapter 6

Ergodicity and Mixing of Seasonal Markov Modulation

The slow seasonal component S_m(t) was modelled as a continuous‑time Markov chain on the finite state space \mathcal{M} = \{m_1, \dots, m_N\}. We now prove its ergodic properties, which guarantee that long‑term averages of feedstock parameters are well‑defined and can be learned.

Assumption 6.1. The generator Q of the chain is irreducible and all states are aperiodic. Equivalently, there exists t_0>0 such that the transition probability matrix P(t) = e^{tQ} has strictly positive entries for all t>t_0.

Theorem 6.1 (Exponential ergodicity)

There exists a unique stationary distribution \pi \in \mathbb{R}^N satisfying \pi^\top Q = 0, \pi_i > 0, and constants C>0, \rho>0 such that for all initial distributions \mu and all t\ge 0,

\|\mu^\top P(t) - \pi^\top\|_{TV} \le C e^{-\rho t},

where \|\cdot\|_{TV} denotes total variation distance.

Proof. This is a standard result for finite‑state, irreducible, aperiodic continuous‑time Markov chains (see Norris, 1998, Theorem 3.8.1). The exponential rate \rho is the absolute value of the largest non‑zero eigenvalue of Q. \square

Corollary 6.2 (Ergodicity of time averages)

Let g : \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R} be any function. Then, almost surely,

\lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T g(S_m(t))\,dt = \sum_{i=1}^N g(m_i) \pi_i.

Proof. Since the chain is stationary and ergodic (the stationary process is ergodic because of irreducibility), Birkhoff’s ergodic theorem (for continuous‑time stationary processes) directly gives the result. Alternatively, one can use the exponential mixing bound to prove the convergence in probability and then upgrade to almost sure via Borel–Cantelli. \square

Corollary 6.3 (Well‑defined long‑term feedstock moments). For the seasonal feedstock component, the time‑averaged mean and second moment exist deterministically:

\bar{S}_m = \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T S_m(t)\,dt = \sum_i m_i \pi_i, \quad
\overline{S_m^2} = \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T S_m(t)^2\,dt = \sum_i m_i^2 \pi_i.

These are constants independent of the random realisation.

Remark 6.4 (Combined process). Because the Poisson component is non‑stationary but has a bounded rate, its time averages may or may not converge. However, the Markov modulation imparts a periodic structure (e.g., seasonal rhythms) that, when averaged over a year, yields a well‑defined ensemble of daily patterns. For control purposes, we may use the stationary distribution to compute the expected long‑term behaviour, while online algorithms adapt to the actual realisation.

