Chapter 7

Mass Conservation on the Network Graph

We now shift from a single digester to a network of n digesters interconnected by a substrate‑sharing infrastructure. The network is modelled as a directed graph \mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}) with n vertices (digesters) and m edges (pipes or transport links). The physical exchange of substrate slurry is governed by mass conservation laws, which we formalise in terms of the graph incidence matrix.

Definition 7.1 (Incidence matrix). Assign an arbitrary orientation to each edge. The node‑edge incidence matrix B \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times m} is defined by

B_{ie} = \begin{cases}
-1, & \text{if node } i \text{ is the tail of edge } e, \\
+1, & \text{if node } i \text{ is the head of edge } e, \\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

A fundamental property is that the column sums of B are zero, i.e.,

\mathbf{1}^\top B = 0,
\tag{7.1}

where \mathbf{1} = (1,\dots,1)^\top.

Let x_i(t) be the total substrate mass (solid‑equivalent) stored at digester i. Its rate of change is the sum of:

· net inflow from other nodes via the transport edges,
· external addition (fresh feedstock from the household) or removal (consumption by the digester’s biology).

Let f_e(t) be the mass flow rate (kg/h) through edge e in the direction of its assigned orientation. A positive f_e means flow from tail to head. The vector of internal flows is f = (f_1, \dots, f_m)^\top. Then the internal contribution to the mass balance at vertex i is the row i of B f: inflow minus outflow along incident edges.

External additions and subtractions are collected in a vector u(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n, where u_i(t) is the net external flow into node i (feedstock added, minus substrate biologically consumed and methane vented, which permanently removes mass from the liquid phase). The complete network dynamics are

\dot{x}(t) = B f(t) + u(t),
\qquad x(0) = x_0 \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^n.
\tag{7.2}

Theorem 7.1 (Global mass conservation). For any flow vector f(t) and any external input u(t), the total mass M(t) = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i(t) = \mathbf{1}^\top x satisfies

\frac{dM}{dt} = \mathbf{1}^\top u(t).
\tag{7.3}

In particular, if there is no net external input (\mathbf{1}^\top u = 0), total mass is a constant of motion.

Proof. Multiply both sides of (7.2) by \mathbf{1}^\top:

\mathbf{1}^\top \dot{x} = \mathbf{1}^\top B f + \mathbf{1}^\top u.

Using the incidence matrix property \mathbf{1}^\top B = 0, the first term vanishes identically. Hence

\frac{d}{dt} (\mathbf{1}^\top x) = \mathbf{1}^\top u.

\square

Remark 7.2. The internal routing matrix B f only redistributes mass; it cannot create or destroy it. This is the essential reason why the network can be analysed as a conservative transport system. Together with the ISS property of each digester, it implies that total mass can only grow because of external feedstock addition, and overall network stability will be determined by the balance between addition and biological conversion.

Corollary 7.3 (Boundedness under bounded inputs). Suppose each digester’s biological consumption rate is non‑negative and bounded (e.g., proportional to V and X). Then the external vector can be decomposed as u = u_{\text{feed}} - u_{\text{conv}}, where u_{\text{feed}} is the feedstock supply and u_{\text{conv}} the consumption. If the total feedstock supply is bounded and the consumption is positive, the total mass is uniformly bounded.

Proof sketch. The biological removal terms (e.g., the Y V - q_{\text{out}} term for gas, but that removes mass from the liquid) are accounted in each node’s u_i. Summing yields that total mass grows linearly with input but is offset by the total conversion, which is a function of positive states and hence has a positive lower bound when states are large, preventing limsup from diverging.

Chapter 8

Exponential Consensus via Laplacian Spectral Gap

The internal redistribution of substrate across the network should balance surpluses and deficits as quickly as possible while conserving total mass. We now analyse a diffusion‑type protocol in which the flow along each edge is proportional to the difference in node potentials.

Let x_i(t) denote the amount of substrate at digester i. The internal network dynamics are given by the conservation equation (7.2):

\dot{x} = B f + u,

with B the incidence matrix of the directed graph \mathcal{G} and f the flow vector. We consider the feedback law

f = -B^\mathsf{T} x,
\tag{8.1}

which assigns a flow from node i to node j proportional to x_i - x_j (if the edge is oriented from i to j). This is analogous to a thermal diffusion or a consensus protocol.

Substituting (8.1) into the mass balance yields the autonomous internal redistribution dynamics

\dot{x} = -B B^\mathsf{T} x = -L x,
\tag{8.2}

where L = B B^\mathsf{T} is the graph Laplacian. If the graph is undirected, L is symmetric positive semi‑definite; for a directed graph one may instead use the out‑degree Laplacian, but the analysis is identical if we symmetrise appropriately. We assume for simplicity that the graph is undirected and connected.

Definition 8.1. The Laplacian matrix L \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n} of an undirected graph \mathcal{G} is defined by L_{ij} = -w_{ij} for i \neq j (if there is an edge with weight w_{ij}), and L_{ii} = \sum_{j \neq i} w_{ij}. L is symmetric, positive semi‑definite, and its nullspace is spanned by \mathbf{1} if and only if \mathcal{G} is connected.

The eigenvalues of L are ordered as 0 = \lambda_1 < \lambda_2 \le \lambda_3 \le \cdots \le \lambda_n. The second smallest eigenvalue \lambda_2 > 0 is called the algebraic connectivity or spectral gap.

Theorem 8.1 (Exponential consensus)

Consider the diffusion dynamics \dot{x} = -L x with initial state x(0) = x_0. Let \bar{x} = \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}^\mathsf{T} x_0 be the average mass. Then for all t \ge 0,

\|x(t) - \bar{x} \mathbf{1}\| \le e^{-\lambda_2 t} \|x_0 - \bar{x} \mathbf{1}\|,
\tag{8.3}

where \|\cdot\| denotes the Euclidean norm. Thus the subsystem converges to the consensus state \bar{x}\mathbf{1} exponentially fast with rate \lambda_2.

Proof. Write x(t) = \bar{x} \mathbf{1} + y(t), where y(t) has zero mean (\mathbf{1}^\mathsf{T} y = 0). Since \mathbf{1} is in the nullspace of L,

\dot{y} = -L y, \quad y(0) = x_0 - \bar{x} \mathbf{1}.

Consider the Lyapunov function V(y) = \frac{1}{2} \|y\|^2. Its derivative is

\dot{V} = y^\mathsf{T} \dot{y} = -y^\mathsf{T} L y \le -\lambda_2 \|y\|^2,

where we used the variational characterisation of \lambda_2: \min_{y \perp \mathbf{1}, \|y\|=1} y^\mathsf{T} L y = \lambda_2. Thus \dot{V} \le -2\lambda_2 V, and integrating gives

\|y(t)\|^2 \le e^{-2\lambda_2 t} \|y(0)\|^2.

Taking square roots yields (8.3). \square

Corollary 8.2 (ISS under external input). With the same diffusion feedback and an external input u(t) that may not be exactly balanced, the closed‑loop system becomes

\dot{x} = -L x + u(t).

The mean \bar{x}(t) evolves as \dot{\bar{x}} = \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}^\mathsf{T} u(t), and the deviation y = x - \bar{x}\mathbf{1} obeys

\|y(t)\| \le e^{-\lambda_2 t} \|y(0)\| + \int_0^t e^{-\lambda_2 (t-s)} \|(I - \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\mathsf{T}) u(s)\| \, ds.

If u(t) is bounded, then y is ultimately bounded.

Proof. Standard ISS argument for linear systems; projection of u onto the consensus subspace only affects the average. The deviation is exponentially stable with a bounded disturbance. \square

This spectral gap result underpins the rapid balancing of substrate across a connected network. In practice, the graph may have random missing edges due to road closures or node failures. We therefore require a graph model and connectivity guarantees, which is the subject of the next chapter.

