Chapter 9

Percolation and Connectivity of Random Substrate‑Sharing Graphs

The physical substrate‑sharing network is not a regular grid; it is a random graph whose edges represent viable transport links between households. Links may be absent due to distance, terrain, or social agreement, and some nodes may be inoperable. We model the network as an inhomogeneous random graph and determine conditions under which a giant connected component exists and the algebraic connectivity is strictly positive with high probability.

Model 9.1 (Random link activation). Let there be n digester nodes located at positions \mathbf{r}_i \in \mathbb{R}^2 (e.g., village coordinates). An undirected edge (i,j) exists with probability

p_{ij} = \varphi( \|\mathbf{r}_i - \mathbf{r}_j\| ),

independently across pairs, where \varphi : \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to [0,1] is a non‑increasing connectivity function. A typical choice is \varphi(d) = \exp(-d^2/(2\sigma^2)) (soft geometric graph) or a hard threshold \varphi(d) = \mathbf{1}_{\{d \le R\}}. The resulting graph \mathcal{G} is a random graph with independent edges.

Definition 9.1. The expected adjacency matrix A has entries A_{ij} = p_{ij} for i \ne j and A_{ii}=0. The expected degree of node i is d_i = \sum_j p_{ij}. The spectral radius \rho(A) is the largest eigenvalue of the matrix A.

A classic result in random graph theory states that a giant connected component emerges when the expected average degree exceeds unity. For inhomogeneous graphs, a spectral criterion is available.

Proposition 9.1 (Giant component via spectral radius)

Consider the random graph \mathcal{G}(n,\{p_{ij}\}) described above, with p_{ij} such that \rho(A) > 1 + \varepsilon for some \varepsilon > 0. Then, with probability tending to 1 as n \to \infty, the graph contains a unique giant connected component of size \Theta(n). Moreover, there exist constants C, c > 0 depending on the distribution such that the size of the largest component N_{\max} satisfies

\mathbb{P}\bigl( N_{\max} \le c n \bigr) \le e^{-C n}.

Proof sketch. This is a specialisation of the results by Chung and Lu (2002) for random graphs with given expected degrees, or the more general theory of inhomogeneous random graphs (Bollobás, Janson, Riordan 2007). The condition \rho(A) > 1 ensures that the expected branching process of node exploration is supercritical, hence a giant component emerges. Concentration follows from large deviation bounds. \square

The effect of random edges on the algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(L) can be quantified using spectral graph theory. When the graph is dense enough, \lambda_2 is of order \Theta(n). However, even for sparse connected graphs, \lambda_2 is positive.

Proposition 9.2 (Algebraic connectivity of random geometric graphs)

Assume a hard threshold connectivity R > 0 and nodes distributed uniformly in a square of area A. Let p = \pi R^2 n / A be the expected average degree. If p > p_c = 1 (the percolation threshold), then with high probability the graph is connected and

\lambda_2(L) \ge c(n) > 0.

For large n, a lower bound is \lambda_2 \ge \frac{p - 1}{C} R^{-2} for some constant C (e.g., from Rayleigh quotient estimates).

Proof. See Penrose (2003) for connectivity threshold and Nyquist (2006) for spectral gap estimates of random geometric graphs. The result guarantees exponential consensus with high probability.

Corollary 9.3 (Practical connectivity). For the biogas network, we only need a connected component that includes the vast majority of nodes. By Proposition 9.1, a spectral radius \rho(A) > 1 suffices for a giant component. Isolated small subgraphs can be handled by local storage or manual transfer. The exponential rate \lambda_2 of the giant component is still positive, ensuring that the bulk of the network equilibrates rapidly.

