Chapter 10

Distributed ADMM for Optimal Substrate Routing

We now address the problem of minimising the total cost of substrate transport across the network while satisfying mass balance constraints. This is a convex network flow problem that must be solved in a fully distributed manner, using only neighbour‑to‑neighbour communication.

Problem 10.1 (Optimal routing). For a given vector of net substrate demands d_i at each node (surplus if d_i>0, deficit if d_i<0), find the edge flows f_{ij} minimising

\min_{f} \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{E}} c_{ij}(f_{ij}) \quad\text{s.t.}\quad \sum_{j: (j,i)\in\mathcal{E}} f_{ji} - \sum_{j: (i,j)\in\mathcal{E}} f_{ij} = d_i \quad \forall i,
\tag{10.1}

where c_{ij} are convex, closed proper, and lower semicontinuous cost functions (e.g., c_{ij}(f) = \kappa_{ij} |f| or c_{ij}(f) = \frac{\kappa_{ij}}{2} f^2). The variables f_{ij} are unrestricted in sign (a negative value indicates flow opposite to the reference orientation). Constraints can be rewritten as B f = d using the incidence matrix B (with appropriate orientation).

Because the problem is separable across edges but coupled through the node balance constraints, it is ideally suited for the Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers (ADMM). We present a distributed algorithm where each node maintains its own local copy of incident flows.

ADMM formulation. Split the flow variables: for each edge (i,j), define two local variables f_{i\to j} at node i and f_{i\leftarrow j} at node j, with the consensus constraint f_{i\to j} = - f_{i\leftarrow j}. The node‑edge incident relations are expressed using the node‑incidence matrices. Equivalently, one can use a node‑based formulation where each node i holds a vector of flows to its neighbours, and the global flow is obtained by agreeing on edge variables. We adopt the edge‑oriented ADMM (Boyd et al. 2011).

Let f_e be the flow on edge e, with an arbitrary but fixed orientation. Node i is incident to a set of edges \mathcal{E}_i; for each edge e\in\mathcal{E}_i, node i stores a local copy z_{i,e}. The copies of the two endpoints satisfy z_{i,e} = f_e and z_{j,e} = -f_e (depending on orientation). The consensus constraints are written as

z_{i,e} = f_e \quad \text{and} \quad z_{j,e} = -f_e,

or more compactly with a signed incidence indicator.

Assumption 10.1. The graph \mathcal{G} is connected and undirected. The cost functions c_{ij} are strictly convex, closed proper, and differentiable with Lipschitz continuous gradients (or we can handle non‑smooth via subgradients). The demands satisfy \sum_i d_i = 0 (feasibility).

Theorem 10.1 (ADMM convergence to optimal routing)

Consider the ADMM algorithm applied to Problem 10.1 with the consensus constraint on edge variables. Let \rho > 0 be the penalty parameter. The iterates \{f_e^k\} (global edge flows) and the local copies \{z_{i,e}^k\} converge as k\to\infty to the unique optimal flow f^* and the consensus values, respectively. Moreover, the primal‑dual gap converges to zero.

Proof sketch.
We cast Problem 10.1 into the form

\min \sum_e c_e(f_e) + I_{\mathcal{C}}(z) \quad \text{s.t. } f_e = z_{i,e} \ (\text{or } \pm), \text{ and } B_{\text{node}} z = d,

where I_{\mathcal{C}} is an indicator function for node balance constraints. This is a convex problem with separable objective across edges and consensus constraints.

The standard ADMM steps (scaled form) are:

\begin{aligned}
f_e^{k+1} &:= \operatorname*{arg\,min}_{f} \left( c_e(f) + \frac{\rho}{2} \sum_{\text{nodes } i:e\in\mathcal{E}_i} \bigl( f - z_{i,e}^k + u_{i,e}^k \bigr)^2 \right), \\
z_{i,e}^{k+1} &:= \text{projection onto balance constraint at node } i, \text{ using the updated } f_e^{k+1}, \\
u_{i,e}^{k+1} &:= u_{i,e}^k + f_e^{k+1} - z_{i,e}^{k+1}.
\end{aligned}

The f‑update decouples per edge and can be solved analytically for quadratic or absolute value costs. The z‑update requires each node to adjust its local copies to satisfy its flow balance \sum_{e\in\mathcal{E}_i} \operatorname{sign}_{i,e} z_{i,e} = d_i while staying as close as possible to the consensus target; this is a projection onto an affine subspace, solvable in closed form or via simple Lagrange multiplier the node can compute locally.

Because the objective is convex, the graph is connected, and the constraints are linear, ADMM converges in the sense that the primal residuals \|f_e^k - z_{i,e}^k\| \to 0, dual residuals \|z_{i,e}^{k+1} - z_{i,e}^k\| \to 0, and the objective value converges to the optimum. The Lagrange multipliers u_{i,e} converge to optimal dual variables associated with the consensus constraints. Furthermore, these dual variables can be interpreted as marginal prices for the substrate exchanges, establishing the connection to a competitive market equilibrium (Chapter 12).

A rigorous proof invokes the equivalence of ADMM to the Douglas–Rachford splitting on the dual problem, or to the convergence of proximal point algorithms (Eckstein & Bertsekas 1992, Boyd et al. 2011). The strict convexity of c_e ensures uniqueness of the primal solution. \square

Remark 10.2 (Communication pattern). Each iteration requires node i to broadcast its current consensus variables for incident edges to neighbours, and receive theirs. This is a purely local message‑passing scheme that respects the physical communication constraints of a rural environment.

Remark 10.3 (Robustness to time‑varying demands). In practice, the net demands d_i(t) vary slowly as feedstock arrivals and biological consumption fluctuate. The ADMM can be run in a receding‑horizon mode using warm‑starting, or one can analyse a dynamic version where the optimisation tracks time‑varying parameters. The extension to uniformly connected time‑varying graphs is the subject of Chapter 11.

Chapter 11

Distributed ADMM under Time‑Varying Uniformly Connected Graphs

The substrate‑sharing network of Chapter 10 was assumed static; yet in practice, transport links may temporarily fail due to weather, road conditions, or seasonal agreements. The optimisation algorithm must be robust to a communication graph that changes over time. We extend the ADMM convergence theory to sequences of graphs that are connected only over bounded intervals — the uniform joint connectivity condition.

11.1 Problem Formulation with Time‑Varying Graphs

Let the time horizon be discrete, indexed by iteration counter k = 0,1,2,\dots. At each k, the set of available communication/transport links is represented by an undirected graph \mathcal{G}^k = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}^k), where \mathcal{V} is the fixed set of n nodes and \mathcal{E}^k \subseteq \mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}. The objective remains the same static convex optimal flow problem (Problem 10.1), but the information exchange between nodes is restricted to the edges present at step k. The ADMM algorithm is modified so that each node updates its local variables using messages received only from neighbours in \mathcal{G}^k.

11.2 Uniform Joint Connectivity

Definition 11.1 (Uniform joint connectivity). A sequence of graphs \{\mathcal{G}^k\}_{k=0}^\infty is said to be uniformly jointly connected (or B-connected) if there exists an integer B \ge 1 such that for every time window of length B, the union graph

\mathcal{G}_{[k,\,k+B-1]} := \bigcup_{\ell = k}^{k+B-1} \mathcal{G}^\ell

is connected. That is, the cumulative graph over any B consecutive steps contains a spanning tree.

This condition is minimal for consensus to be possible, and it also suffices for the convergence of distributed optimisation algorithms with appropriately damped updates.

11.3 ADMM Algorithm on Time‑Varying Graphs

Let the variables be the edge flows f_e and the local copies z_{i,e} as before. Each iteration k:

· Node i receives from active neighbour j the current values z_{j,e}^{k} and dual variables u_{j,e}^{k} for shared edge e.
· Performs a local ADMM update using only links in \mathcal{E}^k. The f_e update can be carried out jointly if nodes communicate; alternatively, a variant where each node updates its own incident variables sequentially can be employed.

We adopt the distributed ADMM with edge‑based consensus (or node‑based) as in Chapter 10, but now the updates are applied only over the edges present at iteration k. For edges not present, the primal and dual variables are left unchanged.

Theorem 11.1 (ADMM convergence under uniform joint connectivity)

Assume the following:

1. The static convex problem (10.1) has a unique optimal solution (costs strictly convex, smooth with Lipschitz gradients, or non‑smooth but handled via subgradients).
2. The graph sequence \{\mathcal{G}^k\} is uniformly jointly connected with window length B.
3. The ADMM step size \rho > 0 is chosen sufficiently large (or a diminishing stepsize variant is used).
4. The initial conditions are arbitrary but finite.

Then the primal variables \{f_e^k\} converge to the unique optimal flow f^*, the consensus constraints are eventually satisfied, and the dual variables converge to optimal Lagrange multipliers, as k\to\infty.

Proof outline.
We use a Lyapunov‑based approach developed for consensus and subgradient algorithms on time‑varying graphs (Nedic & Ozdaglar, 2009; Shi et al., 2015). The ADMM iterates can be cast as a combination of local averaging (over the current graph) and a proximal step. Under the uniform connectivity assumption, the network’s adjacency matrix is not required to be connected at each step; instead, the product of the state transition matrices over a window of length B is a contraction toward the consensus subspace.

More concretely, let w^k be a vector stacking all local copies z_{i,e} and their differences from the consensus values. The update rule can be expressed as

w^{k+1} = \Phi(k) w^k + \text{perturbation due to cost gradient}.

The matrices \Phi(k) are row‑stochastic with positive diagonals, and their composition over any B-length window is scrambling (i.e., the matrix has a column with all entries strictly positive, implying a contraction in the metric of variation). The perturbation term diminishes as consensus is approached. By standard fixed‑point arguments and using the convexity of the cost, one shows that the primal‑dual iterates converge to the saddle point of the Lagrangian.

For the specific case of ADMM, a more tailored proof can be built by interpreting the algorithm as a Douglas–Rachford splitting on a time‑varying graph: the reflection operators are applied only on active links, but the overall operator is still firmly non‑expansive and the iterates are bounded. Using the joint connectivity property, one can prove that no non‑consensus fixed point persists, and the sequence converges to the global minimizer. (Complete detailed proof appears in, e.g., Aybat & Hamedani, 2016, for distributed ADMM on time‑varying networks.) \square

Remark 11.2 (Practicality). The algorithm tolerates intermittent connectivity: a farmer’s digester may go offline for days, but once reconnected, the optimisation resumes. The window length B must be chosen conservatively, but the convergence rate degrades gracefully with B.

