Chapter 12

Economic Equilibrium Interpretation: Lagrange Multipliers as Substrate Prices

The distributed optimisation algorithm does more than route substrate efficiently; it automatically discovers the fair economic value of waste and fertiliser. We now formalise the equivalence between the ADMM dual variables and competitive equilibrium prices in a substrate exchange market.

12.1 The Primal Problem as a Resource Allocation Market

Consider the network flow problem (10.1). Introduce a dual variable (price) p_i \in \mathbb{R} for the mass balance constraint at node i:

\sum_{j:(j,i)\in\mathcal{E}} f_{ji} - \sum_{j:(i,j)\in\mathcal{E}} f_{ij} = d_i.

The Lagrangian of the problem, ignoring edge capacity constraints for simplicity, is

\mathcal{L}(f, p) = \sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{E}} c_{ij}(f_{ij}) + \sum_{i=1}^n p_i \Bigl( d_i - \sum_{j:(i,j)} f_{ij} + \sum_{j:(j,i)} f_{ji} \Bigr).

By rearranging sums, we get

\mathcal{L} = \sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{E}} \bigl[ c_{ij}(f_{ij}) - (p_i - p_j) f_{ij} \bigr] + \sum_{i=1}^n p_i d_i.

Thus, the optimal flow for given prices p minimises the edge cost minus the price differential times flow. The dual function is

g(p) = \min_{f} \mathcal{L}(f,p),

and the dual problem maximises g(p) over p. Strong duality holds because the problem is convex and feasible.

12.2 Competitive Equilibrium

A Walrasian equilibrium for the substrate market consists of a price vector p^* \in \mathbb{R}^n and an allocation (flows) f^* such that:

1. Profit maximisation: For each edge (i,j), the flow f_{ij}^* maximises the net surplus: given the price difference p_j^* - p_i^*, the chosen flow minimises c_{ij}(f) - (p_i^* - p_j^*)f.
2. Market clearing: For each node, the net inflow equals the demand d_i.

If a flow‑price pair satisfies these conditions, it is a competitive equilibrium.

Theorem 12.1 (ADMM finds equilibrium prices)

The ADMM iterates of Chapter 10 (or the time‑varying version of Chapter 11) generate sequences of dual variables \{u_{i,e}^k\} that converge, as k\to\infty, to optimal Lagrange multipliers p_i^* associated with the node balance constraints. Moreover, at convergence, the pair (f^*, p^*) is a Walrasian equilibrium of the substrate market:

· The flow on edge (i,j) maximises  (p_i^* - p_j^*) f - c_{ij}(f) .
· The market clears: \sum_{j} f_{ij}^* - \sum_{j} f_{ji}^* = d_i for all i.

Proof.
In the ADMM formulation with consensus constraints, the Lagrange multipliers u_{i,e} for the edge‑consensus constraints z_{i,e} = \pm f_e converge to the dual variables of those constraints. The node balance multiplier p_i emerges as the sum of appropriate edge multipliers. Standard convex duality (Rockafellar 1970) ensures that the primal‑dual optimum corresponds exactly to the stationary point of the Lagrangian, which characterises the equilibrium. The first‑order optimality condition for the edge cost gives: c_{ij}'(f_{ij}^*) = p_i^* - p_j^*. This is precisely the profit maximisation condition: the marginal cost of transporting one unit equals the spatial price difference. \square

Interpretation.
The dual variable p_i can be seen as the shadow price of a unit of substrate at node i. A node with excess feedstock (large d_i > 0, i.e., surplus) will have a low price, encouraging outflow. A node in deficit (negative d_i) will have a high price, attracting inflow. The equilibrium prices balance regional surpluses and deficits. Farmers can use these prices to decide, for instance, whether to accept additional waste from neighbours in exchange for a payment, or whether to invest in extra digester capacity. The ADMM algorithm, operating entirely on local information, computes these prices automatically.

Remark 12.2 (Carbon credit integration). When carbon credits are linked to methane destruction (Chapter 16–18), the node net demand d_i can be augmented by a credit incentive, effectively lowering the effective cost of substrate conversion. The equilibrium price then embeds both the fertiliser value and the climate value.

