Chapter 15

Stability of the Estimator–Controller Loop under Bounded Estimation Error

We now close the loop: the certainty‑equivalence controller uses the state estimate \hat{x} produced by the UKF to compute the control action u = \kappa(\hat{x}). The true plant is ISS with respect to the external inputs and also with respect to an additive perturbation representing the control error due to mismatched feedback. We prove that the interconnected system remains ISS, with the estimation error acting as a bounded disturbance.

15.1 Plant ISS Property and Certainty‑Equivalence Controller

Recall from Chapter 2 that the isolated digester (and by extension each node in the network) admits an ISS Lyapunov function V(x) satisfying

\alpha_1 \|x\|^2 \le V(x) \le \alpha_2 \|x\|^2, \qquad
\dot{V}(x,u) \le -\alpha V(x) + \gamma(\|u\|),
\tag{15.1}

where u \in \mathbb{R}^p is the vector of exogenous inputs (including the manipulated variable, here the gas extraction rate q_{\text{out}}, and the feedstock concentration S_{\text{in}}). For control design, we partition u into the controlled input u_c = q_{\text{out}} and the disturbance d = S_{\text{in}}. A state‑feedback law

u_c = \kappa(x)

has been designed (e.g., a robust PID or a model‑predictive controller) such that the closed loop

\dot{x} = f(x, \kappa(x), d) := F(x, d)

remains ISS with respect to d, i.e., there exists a Lyapunov function V_{\text{cl}}(x) satisfying

\dot{V}_{\text{cl}} \le -\alpha_{\text{cl}} V_{\text{cl}} + \gamma_{\text{cl}}(\|d\|).
\tag{15.2}

Assumption 15.1 (Lipschitz control law). \kappa is Lipschitz continuous on \mathcal{X}: \|\kappa(x) - \kappa(\hat{x})\| \le L_\kappa \|x - \hat{x}\|.

In practice, this holds for any smooth feedback.

15.2 Error‑Induced Perturbation

When only the estimate \hat{x} is available, the applied control is u_c = \kappa(\hat{x}). The true dynamics become

\dot{x} = f(x, \kappa(\hat{x}), d) = f(x, \kappa(x) + e_u, d),

where e_u = \kappa(\hat{x}) - \kappa(x) satisfies \|e_u\| \le L_\kappa \|x - \hat{x}\| := L_\kappa \epsilon_{\text{est}}. Using the ISS property of the plant with respect to the input channel, we can treat e_u as an additional disturbance.

More precisely, write f(x, \kappa(x)+e_u, d) = F(x,d) + \Delta(x, e_u) with \Delta = f(x, \kappa(x)+e_u, d) - f(x, \kappa(x), d). Since f is Lipschitz in u uniformly in x (from Assumption 2.1), there exists L_f such that \|\Delta\| \le L_f \|e_u\| \le L_f L_\kappa \epsilon_{\text{est}}.

Theorem 15.1 (ISS of the certainty‑equivalence closed loop)

Consider the plant (15.1) under the feedback u_c = \kappa(x) satisfying (15.2). Suppose the state estimate \hat{x} provided by the UKF satisfies \|x(t) - \hat{x}(t)\| \le \epsilon with probability \ge 1-\delta for all t \ge T_0 (Proposition 14.2). Then the closed‑loop system with u_c = \kappa(\hat{x}) is practically ISS in probability: there exist a class \mathcal{KL} function \beta, a class \mathcal{K} function \gamma_{\text{cl}}, and a constant \rho(\epsilon) > 0 such that for all t \ge T_0,

\|x(t)\| \le \beta\bigl(\|x(T_0)\|, t-T_0\bigr) + \gamma_{\text{cl}}\bigl( \sup_{\tau \ge T_0} \|d(\tau)\| \bigr) + \rho(\epsilon),

with probability at least 1-\delta.

Proof.
Using the Lyapunov function V_{\text{cl}} for the ideal closed loop, we compute its derivative along the trajectories of the perturbed system:

\begin{aligned}
\dot{V}_{\text{cl}} &= \nabla V_{\text{cl}}(x) \cdot \bigl( F(x,d) + \Delta(x,e_u) \bigr) \\
&\le -\alpha_{\text{cl}} V_{\text{cl}} + \gamma_{\text{cl}}(\|d\|) + \|\nabla V_{\text{cl}}(x)\| \, \|\Delta\|.
\end{aligned}

Since V_{\text{cl}} is quadratic‑like (\alpha_1 \|x\|^2 \le V_{\text{cl}} \le \alpha_2 \|x\|^2) and its gradient satisfies \|\nabla V_{\text{cl}}(x)\| \le 2\alpha_2 \|x\|, we use Young’s inequality:

\|\nabla V_{\text{cl}}\| \, \|\Delta\| \le \frac{\alpha_{\text{cl}}}{2} V_{\text{cl}} + \frac{2\alpha_2^2}{\alpha_1 \alpha_{\text{cl}}} \|\Delta\|^2.

Taking \|\Delta\| \le L_f L_\kappa \epsilon and defining \rho(\epsilon) = \frac{2\alpha_2^2}{\alpha_1 \alpha_{\text{cl}}} (L_f L_\kappa \epsilon)^2, we obtain

\dot{V}_{\text{cl}} \le -\frac{\alpha_{\text{cl}}}{2} V_{\text{cl}} + \gamma_{\text{cl}}(\|d\|) + \rho(\epsilon).

Applying the comparison lemma yields the practical ISS estimate. The probabilistic qualifier arises because the bound \epsilon on the estimation error itself holds with probability 1-\delta. \square

Remark 15.2 (Network extension). When each digester is equipped with such an estimator–controller loop, and the network coupling is via the ADMM substrate flows, the overall system inherits a network ISS property that can be analysed using the small‑gain conditions developed in Chapter 17.

This theorem seals the gap between estimation and control: even with imperfect knowledge of the state, the closed‑loop system remains robustly stable, with a tunable ultimate bound dictated by the estimator accuracy.

