This thesis has constructed, from the ground up, a rigorous mathematical theory that certifies the feasibility and resilience of a globally scaled, decentralised biogas infrastructure. Starting with a single digester, we proved positivity, dissipativity, and ISS. We formalised the stochastic nature of household waste streams and established their statistical tractability. We modelled the network as a graph, proved mass conservation and rapid consensus via Laplacian spectral gap, and devised a distributed optimisation algorithm that converges under intermittent connectivity and whose dual variables are revealed to be competitive equilibrium prices. We then developed a nonlinear observability analysis, a moment‑matching state estimator, and a separation principle that guarantees robust stability under bounded estimation error. The final theoretical contribution is a global ISS theorem that certifies, with arbitrarily high probability, the long‑term stability of the entire cyber‑physical system of systems.

The work thus transforms the biogas network from an appealing engineering vision into a mathematically proven blueprint. Every theorem carries an explicit set of verifiable hypotheses; no hidden assumptions remain. The framework is ready for implementation and provides the formal guarantees that regulators and investors demand for planetary‑scale deployment.

