Appendix C: Small‑Gain Verification for the Digester Network

We provide a concrete small‑gain calculation. Each digester’s ISS gain from a step change in net inflow \Delta d to the state deviation is approximately \gamma_{\text{dig}}(s) = \frac{L_f L_\kappa}{\alpha_{\text{cl}}} s. The ADMM redistribution gain from a node’s surplus to the allocated flow is \gamma_{\text{ADMM}}(s) = \eta s, where \eta \le 1 due to the contractive property of the flow optimisation (the map from surplus to assigned flow is non‑expansive). The composition is \gamma_{\text{dig}} \circ \gamma_{\text{ADMM}} (s) = \frac{L_f L_\kappa \eta}{\alpha_{\text{cl}}} s. Given the numerical values from Appendix B, \frac{L_f L_\kappa \eta}{\alpha_{\text{cl}}} \approx 0.21 < 1; hence the small‑gain condition holds with a comfortable margin.

In the stochastic setting, these gains are interpreted as expected amplification factors, and the probabilistic small‑gain theorem (see, e.g., Teel, 1998, Theorem 3.1) guarantees ISS of the interconnection when the product is less than one.

